#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093230} :activity/id #uuid "e6f89105-7e4b-5912-9b01-20e1a6810bb5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049755 :topic/id #uuid "1125135d-4341-5e5d-9065-381cc0460203" :topic/math-academy-id 942} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093195 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093471 :tutorial/content "Consider the following polynomials:\n\n$$\nP(x) = 2x^{\\color{blue}4} - 3x + 1, \\qquad\nQ(x) = -6x^{\\color{red}3} + 5x^2 + x\n$$\n\nNotice that these polynomials have degrees $4$ and $3,$ respectively. We write this as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{deg}(P) = {\\color{blue}4}, \\qquad\n\\textrm{deg}(Q) = {\\color{red}3}.\n$$\n\nFurthermore, \n\n- the leading term in $P(x)$ is $2x^{\\color{blue}4},$ and\n\n- the leading term in $Q(x)$ is $-6x^{\\color{red}3}$\n\nAs a result, the leading term in the product $P \\cdot Q$ equals the product of the leading terms of $P$ and $Q\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n2x^{\\color{blue}4} \\cdot (-6x^{\\color{red}3}) = -12x^{{\\color{blue}4}+{\\color{red}3}} = -12x^{\\color{purple}7}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P \\cdot Q$ must be ${\\color{purple}{7}}.$\n\nWe have the following general rule:\n\n*The degree of the product of two polynomials equals the sum of the degrees of these polynomials:* \n\n$$\n\\textrm{deg}(P \\cdot Q) = \\textrm{deg}(P) + \\textrm{deg}(Q)\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "60187490-7cd6-566d-ab45-128e2c4f81c3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6201 :tutorial/title "The Degree of a Product of Two Polynomials"} :step/id #uuid "f1dac5f5-12ea-56be-8067-1b9f815159f5" :step/math-academy-id 37591 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093196}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093196 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092312 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092159 :question/id #uuid "d0f6fda4-4083-5541-8f0e-9a61f740ba8b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9981"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f497212-3946-51e1-9c39-45284ab55da6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049729}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092711} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292688} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385158} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525010}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Degree of a Product of Polynomials"} :step/id #uuid "7e843dd4-2f8a-5237-87b2-28b5a09adb3d" :step/math-academy-id 37594 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093197}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093197 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093472 :tutorial/content "Let's now consider the degree of the sum and difference of two polynomials $P$ and $Q.$ \n\nFirst, let's suppose that $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ have *different* degrees. In this case, the degree of the sum or difference equals the largest of the two degrees. We write\n\n$$\n\\textrm{deg}(P \\pm Q) = \\max \\big\\{ \\textrm{deg}(P), \\textrm{deg}(Q) \\big\\},\n$$\n\n \nwhere the $\\textrm{max}$ function simply returns the largest element of the set.\n\nFor example, suppose that $\\textrm{deg}(P) = 5$ and $\\textrm{deg}(Q) = 2.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{deg}(P + Q) = \\max \\big\\{ 5, 2 \\big\\} = 5.\n$$\n\n \n\nTo see why this is true, note that the leading term of $P$ contains an $x^5,$ and the leading term of $Q$ has an $x^2.$ When we add the two polynomials, no term of $Q$ could cancel out the leading term of $P.$ So the sum of the polynomials must have degree $5.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "fae91d47-b786-5c2f-9a29-dfb06624930f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6202 :tutorial/title "Degrees of Sums and Differences of Polynomials: Case 1"} :step/id #uuid "82947aff-7f67-51bc-9a88-6e5d204e1c08" :step/math-academy-id 37596 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093199}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093198 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093473 :tutorial/content "Polynomials are also closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication but not closed under division.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the following two polynomials:\n\n$$\nP(x) = x+1, \\qquad Q(x) = x^2\n$$\n\n- When we *add* the two polynomials, we get a polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P+Q & =(x+1)+({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =x+1+{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+x+1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- When we *subtract* the two polynomials, we get a polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P-Q & =(x+1)-({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =x+1-{x}^{2} \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}+x+1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- When we *multiply* the two polynomials, we get a polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P\\cdot Q & =(x+1)\\cdot ({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =x\\cdot {x}^{2}+1\\cdot {x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- However, *dividing* the two polynomials does *not* give a polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{P}{Q} & =\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch Out!** Although polynomials are not closed under division, the ratio of two polynomials is *sometimes* a polynomial. For example, if $P=x^2$ and $Q=x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{P}{Q} =  \\dfrac{x^2}{x} = x,\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial." :tutorial/id #uuid "d529e51a-742c-52f5-874b-546cf98a1849" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6228 :tutorial/title "Closure Properties of Polynomials"} :step/id #uuid "2236e99f-a53e-57c2-91ab-c3091e8a86a9" :step/math-academy-id 37829 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093231}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093199 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093474 :tutorial/content "Now suppose that $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ have the *same* degree. That is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{deg}(P) =   \\textrm{deg}(Q) = n.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn this case, the degree of the sum or difference can be *any integer* from $0$ to $n.$\n\n$$\n0 \\leq \\textrm{deg}(P \\pm Q) \\leq n\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's consider some examples:\n\n- Let $P$ and $Q$ be polynomials of degree $15.$ Then, the *largest possible degree* of $P+Q$ is $15$ since the resulting polynomial can't contain terms of order higher than $15.$\n\n- On the other hand, the *smallest possible degree* of $P+Q$ is $0.$ For instance, suppose $P=x^{15}$ and $Q=1-x^{15}.$ Then $P +Q = 1,$ which is a polynomial of degree $0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f9d9fe12-e873-5b5b-aca8-f212f9be62b4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6229 :tutorial/title "Degrees of Sums and Differences of Polynomials: Case 2"} :step/id #uuid "6b4c7081-0037-5d22-8c22-bf18957c01d2" :step/math-academy-id 37830 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093232}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093230 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093380 :tutorial/content "When we add, subtract, or multiply two integers, the answer is always an integer. \n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the following two integers:\n\n$$\na = 3, \\qquad b=2\n$$\n\n- When we *add* the two integers, we get an integer:\n\n$$\na + b = 3+2 = 5\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\n- When we *subtract* the two integers, we get an integer:\n\n$$\na - b = 3-2 = 1\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\n- When we *multiply* the two integers, we get an integer:\n\n$$\na \\cdot b = 3\\cdot 2 = 6\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\n- However, dividing the two integers does *not* give an integer:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac32 \\quad{\\color{red}{\\boldsymbol\\times}}\n$$\n\nWe say the integers are **closed** under addition, subtraction, and multiplication but **not closed** under division.\n\n**Watch Out!** Although integers are not closed under division, the ratio of two integers is *sometimes* an integer. For example, if $a=4$ and $b=2,$ then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} =  \\dfrac{4}{2} = 2,\n$$\n\nwhich is an integer." :tutorial/id #uuid "a7d0a253-b58a-5205-b05d-d5d2f1d49f6e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1593 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "90c92812-0369-53f2-8139-3a602c9d8ddf" :step/math-academy-id 4197 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093198}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093231 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092327 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091946 :question/id #uuid "5ae8b304-5ce3-5520-8893-92a02784155d" :question/math-academy-id "e-1316"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eab94cb-5a5a-5915-ab95-963cb4c63817" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049500} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050323} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050827}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092554} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272249} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435053} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525034}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Closure Properties of Polynomials"} :step/id #uuid "11713b46-54cf-58b5-8dc5-2984d413ed5f" :step/math-academy-id 4198 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093195}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093232 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092328 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091947 :question/id #uuid "53557831-390f-5b8b-b5c3-6634d425c04d" :question/math-academy-id "e-1317"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36318568-6dfe-5ba9-b607-a2d269d05425" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092712} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092713} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292689} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329779} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329780} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435056} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525054} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551289}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Constraints on the Degree of a Polynomial"} :step/id #uuid "bd94fe81-ec77-53b8-8ef8-e461ac344d67" :step/math-academy-id 4201 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093233}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093233 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092329 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091948 :question/id #uuid "621d4d9b-ebfb-56ef-9614-807077aea4c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-1318"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a0cc5ea-7f70-5c5f-841e-01a6813da8c6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092714} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247218} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435054} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551335}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Operations on Polynomials"} :step/id #uuid "cabcbccd-aabb-5b9e-a421-ce222693f535" :step/math-academy-id 4204}] :activity/title "Closure Properties of Polynomials" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186092437}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551396} :activity/id #uuid "63c240ab-0b82-5953-99c0-b57603d6d46d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049755 :topic/id #uuid "1125135d-4341-5e5d-9065-381cc0460203" :topic/math-academy-id 942} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551371 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551289 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46e5a5c3-60ec-5627-a225-2932362ec397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550535} {:answer/id #uuid "8c9d1adf-aac7-5855-ae2f-e140a9ae5328" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550724} {:answer/id #uuid "a723bf8c-84a9-5f13-a1ce-a539ed90eb0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550808} {:answer/id #uuid "ad2db5e7-ef23-5703-aeda-1493ac482354" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550835} {:answer/id #uuid "fc8f5a46-48bc-535e-8332-02ed609bb690" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551047}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550724} :answer-field/id #uuid "a97b574a-ddca-51ce-b670-8bb1bab44ffc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "70cb115a-1a5a-5899-8751-fbde76c6f208" :question/math-academy-id "q-111383" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $5$, what is the highest possible degree of $P+Q?$" :question/worked-solution "The highest possible degree of $P+Q$ is $5.$\n\nFor example, suppose $P={x}^{5}$ and $Q={x}^{5}.$ Then $P+Q=2{x}^{5}.$ \n\nSince neither $P$ nor $Q$ contain terms of order higher than ${x}^{5},$ $P+Q$ can't contain terms of order higher than ${x}^{5}.$"} :step/id #uuid "1b7d2c5a-9dd1-516d-a496-880f67c4ba7e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551396 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551326 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5397c68e-c67c-554c-9f05-a9f7a14e8c85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550567} {:answer/id #uuid "a3939c82-26fd-5cc6-93af-ef355402eb4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550793} {:answer/id #uuid "a9ee9f5a-c959-5e80-ac37-ae91ab167c24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550819} {:answer/id #uuid "f3578614-1ffa-54f6-b421-463ca35d969d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551020} {:answer/id #uuid "f6df2b25-6b78-5f3f-bfc1-4493840b6b27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551030}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551020} :answer-field/id #uuid "efdbde40-612d-561b-91ae-eae8bf3a3a21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551219}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b45d9b53-e4ec-561f-955c-4e4b1f88b215" :question/math-academy-id "q-46426" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$ and $5$, respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P+Q$ is always a polynomial\n\nII. $P\\cdot Q$ is always a polynomial\n\nIII. $\\frac{P}{Q}$ is always a polynomial" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Polynomials are closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. Polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, suppose $P={x}^{3}$ and $Q={x}^{5}.$ Then $\\frac{P}{Q}=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{5}}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},$ which is not a polynomial.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true."} :step/id #uuid "47e18e53-ae77-5c80-aa5d-573b4263a98c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551446}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551446 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551335 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ba58f0f-dfc0-507b-8520-46f049d08857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550399} {:answer/id #uuid "299137ec-d817-5726-82f2-18dfed3ce6ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550470} {:answer/id #uuid "4d08e1d2-4a2e-51a5-96ae-c016f3c20687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550550} {:answer/id #uuid "56caf7ba-915d-5ed9-acd6-ec5f243ce59c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550579} {:answer/id #uuid "5f8ff36c-d673-5223-a9d5-a6b900bddcbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550601}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550601} :answer-field/id #uuid "7abffeb2-f396-51d3-8c57-6dd2f44d6299" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551138}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ceee5cde-40e3-565c-b032-fa1a9c5be489" :question/math-academy-id "q-51691" :question/problem "Given that $P(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $5,$ which of the following statements are always true?\n\nI. $P(x)-4{x}^{6}$ is a polynomial\n\nII. $4{x}^{6}\\cdot P(x)$ is a polynomial\n\nIII. $\\frac{4{x}^{6}}{P(x)}$ is a polynomial" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Polynomials are closed under subtraction.\n\n- Statement II is true. Polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, suppose $P(x)={x}^{5}+1.$ Then $\\frac{4{x}^{6}}{P(x)}=\\frac{4{x}^{6}}{{x}^{5}+1},$ which is not a polynomial.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true."} :step/id #uuid "c430f29a-849f-5c9d-b6d0-59cdd7fd14de" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551371}}] :activity/title "Closure Properties of Polynomials" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550352}]}
